Stick -> mouse motion: apply a response curve (speed = speed_pps * m^k)

InputPlumber 0.81.0 maps a stick to mouse motion linearly: a per-axis 0.20
deadzone, then speed_pps * deflection. That makes small pointer adjustments
hard. This replaces it with a radial deadzone (0.20 of the vector magnitude),
the magnitude rescaled past it to m in [0, 1], and speed_pps * m^k with the
direction kept. Full deflection is still exactly speed_pps (the magnitude is
clamped to 1, so square-gate diagonals are not faster); k = 1 is linear.

k comes from the environment variable INPUTPLUMBER_POINTER_CURVE, read once
at first use (default 2.5; invalid or non-positive values are ignored with a
warning). The math is the pure function pointer_curve(), with unit tests.

Slow speeds stay smooth without a change: MouseDevice::poll already
accumulates the sub-pixel remainder (MouseMotionState::mouse_remainder) and
only emits whole-pixel REL_X/REL_Y deltas.

Not upstream: HinterOS-specific behaviour.

